# How each rucc target is spelled to each tool, and what runs a binary for it.
#
# One record per line, tab separated, in this order:
#
#   tuple  zig  gcc  runner
#
# The tuple is rucc's canonical spelling, which is the one in docs/TARGETS.md in the compiler
# repository and the one `rucc-tuple` parses to. Every other column is somebody else's spelling of
# the same machine, and a dash means that tool has no way to say it.
#
# The zig column was not written from the documentation. Every entry in it was checked by running
# `zig cc -target <spelling> -dM -E -x c /dev/null` against zig 0.16.0, which is the version pinned
# in toolchains/manifest, and the dashes are the ones that failed. Three of them are worth knowing
# about: zig lists illumos as an operating system and `zig cc` cannot target it, because there is
# no libc for it in the distribution; wasm32-wasip1 and later are spelled `wasm32-wasi` with no
# version, so the three rucc rows collapse onto one zig row and rucc's finer distinction has no
# reference to compare against; and arm64ec has no spelling at all, which agrees with
# spec/cross-compile/06-abis.md section 6.8 declining it.
#
# The gcc column is the Debian and Ubuntu cross compiler prefix, so `aarch64-linux-gnu` means
# `aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc` from `gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu`. It is much sparser than the zig column and
# that sparseness is the argument for this whole line of work: a per target gcc has to exist as a
# package before you can compare against it, and for most of this table nobody has built one.
#
# The runner column is what executes a binary for that target on a Linux host. qemu user mode for
# the Linux targets, and a dash everywhere else, because qemu user mode runs Linux binaries and a
# Mach-O or a PE needs a different answer. spec/cross-compile/14-testing.md section 14.6 is the
# rule for reading a result that came from an emulator rather than from hardware.

